# 🧪 Hybrid Graph Attention Network (GAT) for JAK1 Inhibitor Activity Prediction

An end-to-end Cheminformatics and Deep Learning pipeline designed to predict the inhibitory potency ($\text{pIC}_{50}$) of small molecules against **Janus Kinase 1 (JAK1)**. This repository includes the trained model weights, feature scaling artifacts, rigorous QSAR validation scripts (X-Scrambling), and an interactive high-throughput web interface built with Streamlit.

---

## 🔬 Scientific Methodology & Architecture

Traditional Quantitative Structure-Activity Relationship (QSAR) models rely either solely on 2D molecular descriptors/fingerprints or purely on molecular graphs. To maximize predictive power and capture both local chemical environments and global physicochemical properties, this project implements an **Advanced Hybrid Graph Attention Network (`AdvancedHybridGAT`)**:

1. **Graph Representation Branch:**
   * Molecular structures (SMILES) are parsed via `RDKit` into bidirectional graphs where nodes represent atoms (6 physicochemical atom features) and edges represent chemical bonds (6 bond features including conjugation and ring membership).
   * Three sequential multi-head **Graph Attention Layers (`GATConv`)** with `ELU` activations and global mean pooling extract topological embeddings.
2. **Tabular Descriptors Branch:**
   * Extracts 2,048-bit Morgan Fingerprints (radius 2) alongside full 2D RDKit molecular descriptors.
   * Dimensionality reduction via permutation/R²-drop importance retains the top **1,301 informative features**, standardized using a pre-fitted `StandardScaler`.
   * Processed through a dedicated Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) with Batch Normalization and Dropout ($p = 0.4$) to prevent overfitting.
3. **Feature Fusion & Regression:**
   * Graph embeddings and tabular representations are concatenated and passed through a dense regression head to output continuous $\text{pIC}_{50}$ predictions.

---

## 📊 Model Performance & Validation

* **Validation Performance:** Achieved a coefficient of determination of **$R^2 = 0.8701$** on held-out test compounds, demonstrating strong generalization across diverse chemical scaffolds.
* **Rigorous QSAR Validation (X-Scrambling & Feature Auditing):** To rule out chance correlation and ensure model interpretability, `validation_x_scrambling.py` performs 30-iteration global X-scrambling and individual permutation feature importance across all 1,301 descriptors (full results archived in `x_scrambling_7000_1301_report.csv`).
* **Assay Heterogeneity & Biological Context:** In cheminformatics modeling (e.g., ChEMBL-derived datasets), experimental $\text{IC}_{50}$ values vary depending on the assay format (biochemical enzymatic assays vs. cell-based recombinant assays). For instance, benchmark inhibitors like **Tofacitinib** exhibit $\text{pIC}_{50} \approx 6.95$ in biochemical assays ($\text{IC}_{50} \approx 112\text{ nM}$) and $\approx 8.50$ in recombinant enzyme assays ($\text{IC}_{50} \approx 3.2\text{ nM}$). The model faithfully reflects the predominant assay distribution of the curated training set ($\text{predicted pIC}_{50} \approx 7.03$).

